VoDoge Edge -- licensing
========================

Copyright (c) 2026 the VoDoge Edge authors
Source: https://github.com/vodoge/vodoge-edge

This repository is NOT under a single license. It builds two independent sets
of artifacts under two different licenses, and which one applies to you depends
on which directory you took code from. This file is the whole map: read it and
you know what you have.

  SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 AND AGPL-3.0-or-later

Nothing here is legal advice. It is a statement of what this project grants and
of the facts that statement rests on.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Apache-2.0 -- the Rust workspace
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The following paths are licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0. The
complete text is in section 6 of this file.

    contract/       edge-core/      edge-modem/     edge-store/
    edge-uplink/    edge-agent/     edge-panel/     edge-panel-api/
    edge-ui/        edge-proxy/     edge-bin/

Also Apache-2.0: everything at the repository root that is not listed in
section 2 -- Cargo.toml, Cargo.lock, scripts/, docs/, .github/, README.md.

This matches `license = "Apache-2.0"` in Cargo.toml, which is the license
metadata for these eleven crates and only for these eleven crates.

That match is not automatic, and it has already been lost once. The
workspace-level `license` key reaches a crate only when that crate's own
Cargo.toml says `license.workspace = true`. edge-panel-api/ and edge-ui/ joined
the workspace (commits 8979b4f and 0d9df15) without that line, so from those
commits until this correction they were published here carrying no license
metadata at all -- edge-ui/ compiled into the released binary among them --
while this list still said nine. (An earlier draft of this paragraph counted
those commits. A number derived from HEAD does not belong in a permanent
licence file: it was accurate for about ten minutes, and every commit since
has made it more wrong. The two crates also joined five commits apart, so no
single count describes both.) A crate falls under this section only once its
own manifest opts in. Check that line when you add one, and check this list.

    Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 (the "License"); you may not
    use these files except in compliance with the License. You may obtain a
    copy of the License at

        http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0

    Unless required by applicable law or agreed to in writing, software
    distributed under the License is distributed on an "AS IS" BASIS, WITHOUT
    WARRANTIES OR CONDITIONS OF ANY KIND, either express or implied. See the
    License for the specific language governing permissions and limitations
    under the License.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. AGPL-3.0-or-later -- the two Go modules
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The following paths are licensed under the GNU Affero General Public License,
version 3 or (at your option) any later version:

    voice/          the IMS media relay      -> binary: vodoge-voice
    vowifi/         the IKEv2/EAP-AKA stack  -> binary: vodoge-ike-probe

The complete AGPL-3.0 text ships with each of them:

    voice/LICENSE
    vowifi/LICENSE

Both files are byte-for-byte the unmodified Free Software Foundation text
(35182 bytes as checked out here), the same copy that upstream ships.

Why AGPL and not Apache: both modules require github.com/boa-z/vowifi-go
(voice/go.mod, vowifi/go.mod), and that project is AGPL-3.0 with no additional
permission, no linking exception and no dual license. Building either binary
compiles roughly 39,900 lines of that upstream code into it -- about five times
the volume of our own Go code in these two modules. We did not try to argue our
way around that. We took the license.

This is a grant, not a warning: you may use, study, modify and redistribute
voice/ and vowifi/ under AGPL-3.0. It does mean these two modules can no longer
be shipped as closed source, by us or by anyone.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Third-party code that is NOT in this repository
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

github.com/boa-z/vowifi-go is not vendored here. Both go.mod files replace it
with a path pointing outside this repository, at a read-only local mirror of
upstream commit 1e9c6e6adbfcd9667695149d5ecb0f71cd062f07.

That mirror is an exact copy, not a fork: scripts/verify-vendor-mirror.sh
compares every file in it against the upstream commit's git blobs and reports
212/212 matching. Keeping it exact is deliberate -- AGPL-3.0 section 0 defines
"modify" as adapting the work "other than the making of an exact copy", and an
exact copy is what we want it to stay. Do not edit it, and do not build with
"go build -overlay", which would change what the compiler sees while leaving the
files on disk untouched and this check falsely green.

Get the upstream source from https://github.com/boa-z/vowifi-go (commit
1e9c6e6a), or see README.md for how to materialise the mirror.

See NOTICE for attribution.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. Why the Rust workspace is still Apache-2.0 -- and when to stop believing it
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

No Rust crate in this repository depends on any AGPL code. The reason is not a
principle, it is a set of facts about the current shape of this tree: today the
Apache-2.0 Rust artifacts and the AGPL-3.0 Go artifacts are two unrelated
products that never meet.

That is a claim with an expiry date, so it is written below as five conditions
rather than as an assertion. Each one names the check that would falsify it.

    If ANY ONE of these five stops being true, the conclusion in section 1 is
    no longer supported and must be re-examined before the next release. It may
    not survive. Do not treat section 1 as settled without re-running these.

All five were verified against commit 259ebd3 on 2026-08-25.

C1. There is no cgo anywhere in this repository.
    Falsified when this returns any hit in tracked source:
        git grep -nE 'import "C"|//export |#cgo|c-archive|c-shared|buildmode'
    Verified: zero hits. Known future trigger: the AMR-WB transcoding slot
    described at voice/internal/bridge/peer.go:12 is written as "the cgo codec
    drops into the same slot". Installing it breaks C1.

C2. The Go modules build with CGO_ENABLED=0 into two standalone executables.
    They are voice/cmd/vodoge-voice and vowifi/cmd/vodoge-ike-probe, the only
    two "package main" files in the tree.
    Falsified when a Go build here needs cgo, or emits a library (c-archive,
    c-shared, a static or dynamic object) instead of an executable.
    Verified: .github/workflows/ci.yml:109 builds both modules with
    CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=linux GOARCH=amd64.

C3. No Rust source in this repository references either executable.
    It does not spawn them, link them, or speak to them over IPC or a socket.
    Falsified when either of these stops being empty:
        git grep -n 'vodoge-voice\|vodoge-ike-probe' -- '*.rs'
        git grep -n 'Command::new' -- '*.rs'
    Verified: both empty. The only std::process uses in Rust are exit and id;
    nothing is launched.

C4. voice/ and vowifi/ are not part of the Cargo workspace.
    Falsified when Cargo.toml's members list gains either path, or when any
    crate grows a build script or dependency that compiles or embeds them.
    Verified: members lists exactly the eleven crates named in section 1.

C5. Nothing in this repository packages the Rust agent and the Go binaries into
    one deliverable.
    Falsified when a systemd unit, a container image, an installer, or any
    packaging step that ships them together appears.
    Verified: git ls-files matching '\.service$' is empty; there is no systemd
    unit and no combined package anywhere in the tree.

C3 and C5 are the two most likely to break next. The planned supervisor work
(goal card T042) is precisely the step that would wire the Rust agent to these
two Go binaries; that card's stop_if already forbids doing it before this file
is revisited.

When a condition breaks, the question to answer is not "did the Rust code catch
something". It is: are the Apache-2.0 artifacts and the AGPL-3.0 artifacts now
one combined work? Answer that before shipping, not after.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. AGPL-3.0 section 13 -- source for users on the network
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

vodoge-voice and vodoge-ike-probe are intended to let people place calls through
them, which means people will interact with them remotely over a network.
AGPL-3.0 section 13 gives those users the right to receive the Corresponding
Source. This is that offer, and it is deliberate rather than incidental:

    The complete Corresponding Source for these programs is published at

        https://github.com/vodoge/vodoge-edge

    and may be obtained by anyone, at no charge, over the network, without an
    account:

        git clone https://github.com/vodoge/vodoge-edge

    The upstream AGPL dependency compiled into these binaries is at
    https://github.com/boa-z/vowifi-go, commit 1e9c6e6a.

This repository is public on purpose so that the offer above is always
satisfiable. If it is ever made private, section 13 does not go away -- the
source must then be offered to network users by some other standard means, and
that has to be arranged before the repository is closed, not after.

A deployed binary must correspond to a commit that is actually published here.
Do not run a private patch against network users without pushing it.


